window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2021-12-3", "source": "2021年12月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay related to the short passage given below. In your essay, you are to comment on the phenomenon described in the passage and suggest measures to address the issue. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.\n\nSome parents in China are overprotective of their children. They plan everything for their children, make all the decisions for them, and do not allow them to explore on their own in case they make mistakes or get hurt.", "sample": "It is not uncommon nowadays that some parents in China are overprotective of their children. They arrange\neverything, make every decision on their behalf, and forbid any independent exploration for fear of mistakes or\ninjuries. In my view, however well-intentioned this practice may be, it does far more harm than good.\nTo begin with, such excessive protection deprives children of the chance to develop independence. When every\nchoice is made for them, they never learn how to weigh options or solve problems on their own. Moreover,\nbeing shielded from all failure leaves them without resilience; a child who has never fallen simply does not\nknow how to stand up again. As a result, these children may grow into adults who are competent in theory yet\nhelpless in real life.\nTo address this issue, parents should first loosen their grip and let children make age-appropriate decisions by\nthemselves. In addition, they ought to treat mistakes as valuable lessons rather than disasters to be avoided at\nall costs. Ultimately, the wisest parents shift their role from controllers to guides, offering support from behind\ninstead of clearing every obstacle ahead."}, "translation": {"prompt": "井冈山地处湖南、江西两省交界处，因其辉煌的革命历史被誉为\"中国革命红色摇篮\"。1927年10月，毛泽东、朱德等老一辈革命家率领中国工农红军来到这里，开展了艰苦卓绝的斗争，创建了第一个农村革命根据地，点燃了中国革命的星星之火，开辟了 \"农村包围 (besiege) 城市， 武装夺取政权\" 这一具有中国特色的革命道路，中国革命从这里迈向胜利。井冈山现有100多处革命旧址，成为一个\"没有围墙的革命历史博物馆\"， 是爱国主义和革命传统教育的重要基地。", "sample": "Located on the border between Hunan and Jiangxi provinces, the Jinggang Mountains are honored as \"the cradle of the Chinese revolution\" for their glorious revolutionary history. In October 1927, Mao Zedong, Zhu De and other revolutionaries of the older generation led the Chinese Workers' and Peasants' Red Army here, where they waged an arduous struggle, founded the first rural revolutionary base, kindled the spark of the Chinese revolution, and opened up a revolutionary path with Chinese characteristics of \"besieging the cities from the countryside and seizing power by armed force.\" From here, the Chinese revolution marched toward victory. Today the Jinggang Mountains have more than 100 revolutionary sites and have become a \"revolutionary history museum without walls,\" serving as an important base for education in patriotism and revolutionary tradition."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": null, "matchIntro": "Why facts don't change our minds", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The economist J. K. Galbraith once wrote, \"Faced with a choice between changing one's mind and proving there is no need to do so, almost everyone gets busy with the proof.\""}, {"label": "B", "text": "Leo Tolstoy was even bolder: \"The most difficult subjects can be explained to the most slow-witted man if he has not formed any idea of them already; but the simplest thing cannot be made clear to the most intelligent man if he is firmly persuaded that he knows already, without a shadow of a doubt, what is laid before him.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "What's going on here? Why don't facts change our minds? And why would someone continue to believe a false or inaccurate idea anyway? How do such behaviors serve us? Humans need a reasonably accurate view of the world in order to survive. If your model of reality is wildly different from the actual world, then you struggle to take effective actions each day. However, truth and accuracy are not the only things that matter to the human mind. Humans also seem to have a deep desire to belong."}, {"label": "D", "text": "In Atomic Habits, I wrote, \"Humans are herd animals. We want to fit in, to bond with others, and to earn the respect and approval of our peers. Such inclinations are essential to our survival. For most of our evolutionary history, our ancestors lived in tribes. Becoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a death sentence.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "Understanding the truth of a situation is important, but so is remaining part of a tribe. While these two desires often work well together, they occasionally come into conflict. In many circumstances, social connection is actually more helpful to your daily life than understanding the truth of a particular fact or idea. The Harvard psychologist Steven Pinker put it this way, \"People are embraced or condemned according to their beliefs, so one function of the mind may be to hold beliefs that bring the belief-holder the greatest number of allies, protectors, or disciples(信徒), rather than beliefs that are most likely to be true.\""}, {"label": "F", "text": "We don't always believe things because they are correct. Sometimes we believe things because they make us look good to the people we care about. I thought Kevin Simler put it well when he wrote, \"If a brain anticipates that it will be rewarded for adopting a particular belief, it's perfectly happy to do so, and doesn't much care where the reward comes from—whether it's pragmatic(实用主义的) (better outcomes resulting from better decisions), social (better treatment from one's peers), or some mix of the two.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "False beliefs can be useful in a social sense even if they are not useful in a factual sense. For lack of a better phrase, we might call this approach \"factually false, but socially accurate.\" When we have to choose between the two, people often select friends and family over facts. This insight not only explains why we might hold our tongue at a dinner party or look the other way when our parents say something offensive, but also reveals a better way to change the minds of others."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Convincing someone to change their mind is really the process of convincing them to change their tribe. If they abandon their beliefs, they run the risk of losing social ties. You can't expect someone to change their mind if you take away their community too. You have to give them somewhere to go. Nobody wants their worldview torn apart if loneliness is the outcome."}, {"label": "I", "text": "The way to change people's minds is to become friends with them, to integrate them into your tribe, to bring them into your circle. Now, they can change their beliefs without the risk of being abandoned socially."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Perhaps it is not difference, but distance, that breeds tribalism and hostility. As proximity increases, so does understanding. I am reminded of Abraham Lincoln's quote, \"I don't like that man. I must get to know him better.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "Facts don't change our minds. Friendship does. Years ago, Ben Casnocha mentioned an idea to me that I haven't been able to shake: The people who are most likely to change our minds are the ones we agree with on 98 percent of topics. If someone you know, like, and trust believes a radical idea, you are more likely to give it merit, weight, or consideration. You already agree with them in most areas of life. Maybe you should change your mind on this one too. But if someone wildly different than you proposes the same radical idea, well, it's easy to dismiss them as nuts."}, {"label": "L", "text": "One way to visualize this distinction is by mapping beliefs on a spectrum. If you divide this spectrum into 10 units and you find yourself at Position 7, then there is little sense in trying to convince someone at Position 1. The gap is too wide. When you're at Position 7, your time is better spent connecting with people who are at Positions 6 and 8, gradually pulling them in your direction."}, {"label": "M", "text": "The most heated arguments often occur between people on opposite ends of the spectrum, but the most frequent learning occurs from people who are nearby. The closer you are to someone, the more likely it becomes that the one or two beliefs you don't share will bleed over into your own mind and shape your thinking. The further away an idea is from your current position, the more likely you are to reject it outright. When it comes to changing people's minds, it is very difficult to jump from one side to another. You can't jump down the spectrum. You have to slide down it."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Any idea that is sufficiently different from your current worldview will feel threatening. And the best place to ponder a threatening idea is in a non-threatening environment. As a result, books are often a better vehicle for transforming beliefs than conversations or debates. In conversation, people have to carefully consider their status and appearance. They want to save face and avoid looking stupid. When confronted with an uncomfortable set of facts, the tendency is often to double down on their current position rather than publicly admit to being wrong. Books resolve this tension. With a book, the conversation takes place inside someone's head and without the risk of being judged by others. It's easier to be open-minded when you aren't feeling defensive."}, {"label": "O", "text": "There is another reason bad ideas continue to live on, which is that people continue to talk about them. Silence is death for any idea. An idea that is never spoken or written down dies with the person who conceived it. Ideas can only be remembered when they are repeated. They can only be believed when they are repeated. I have already pointed out that people repeat ideas to signal they are part of the same social group. But here's a crucial point most people miss: People also repeat bad ideas when they complain about them. Before you can criticize an idea, you have to reference that idea. You end up repeating the ideas you're hoping people will forget—but, of course, people can't forget them because you keep talking about them. The more you repeat a bad idea, the more likely people are to believe it."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Let's call this phenomenon Clear's Law of Recurrence: The number of people who believe an idea is directly proportional to the number of times it has been repeated during the last year—even if the idea is false."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "The subject of automation and its role in our economy has taken hold in American public discourse. Technology broadly and automation specifically are dramatically reshaping the way we work. And we need to have a plan for what's still to come.\nWe don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of automation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood libraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\nThe statistics in manufacturing are staggering. Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances in technology.\nThat is why more people are criticizing the ever-increasing role of technology in our economy. Our country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with fewer workers. However, it's not just factories that are seeing losses—software and information technology are also having a dramatic impact on jobs most people think are secure from the forces of a rapidly-changing economy. Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on American families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\nSo why are people so insistent on ignoring the perils of automation? They are failing to look ahead at a time when planning for the future is more important than ever. Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before it. I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers makes me skeptical. No one can currently say where the new jobs are coming from or when, and any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives.\nI'm not overstating the danger: look at what's happened to the labor force. According to economic research, one in six working-age men, 25–54, doesn't have a job. Fifty years ago, nearly 100 percent of men that age were working. Women's labor force participation, meanwhile, has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s.\nAmerican families and prominent business leaders are aware that there's a big problem with automation. The value of a college degree is diminishing, and our upward mobility is declining. If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start thinking about how we can rightfully address automation.", "47": "The subject of automation and its role in our economy has taken hold in American public discourse. Technology broadly and automation specifically are dramatically reshaping the way we work. And we need to have a plan for what's still to come.\nWe don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of automation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood libraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\nThe statistics in manufacturing are staggering. Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances in technology.\nThat is why more people are criticizing the ever-increasing role of technology in our economy. Our country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with fewer workers. However, it's not just factories that are seeing losses—software and information technology are also having a dramatic impact on jobs most people think are secure from the forces of a rapidly-changing economy. Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on American families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\nSo why are people so insistent on ignoring the perils of automation? They are failing to look ahead at a time when planning for the future is more important than ever. Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before it. I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers makes me skeptical. No one can currently say where the new jobs are coming from or when, and any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives.\nI'm not overstating the danger: look at what's happened to the labor force. According to economic research, one in six working-age men, 25–54, doesn't have a job. Fifty years ago, nearly 100 percent of men that age were working. Women's labor force participation, meanwhile, has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s.\nAmerican families and prominent business leaders are aware that there's a big problem with automation. The value of a college degree is diminishing, and our upward mobility is declining. If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start thinking about how we can rightfully address automation.", "48": "The subject of automation and its role in our economy has taken hold in American public discourse. Technology broadly and automation specifically are dramatically reshaping the way we work. And we need to have a plan for what's still to come.\nWe don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of automation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood libraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\nThe statistics in manufacturing are staggering. Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances in technology.\nThat is why more people are criticizing the ever-increasing role of technology in our economy. Our country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with fewer workers. However, it's not just factories that are seeing losses—software and information technology are also having a dramatic impact on jobs most people think are secure from the forces of a rapidly-changing economy. Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on American families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\nSo why are people so insistent on ignoring the perils of automation? They are failing to look ahead at a time when planning for the future is more important than ever. Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before it. I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers makes me skeptical. No one can currently say where the new jobs are coming from or when, and any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives.\nI'm not overstating the danger: look at what's happened to the labor force. According to economic research, one in six working-age men, 25–54, doesn't have a job. Fifty years ago, nearly 100 percent of men that age were working. Women's labor force participation, meanwhile, has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s.\nAmerican families and prominent business leaders are aware that there's a big problem with automation. The value of a college degree is diminishing, and our upward mobility is declining. If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start thinking about how we can rightfully address automation.", "49": "The subject of automation and its role in our economy has taken hold in American public discourse. Technology broadly and automation specifically are dramatically reshaping the way we work. And we need to have a plan for what's still to come.\nWe don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of automation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood libraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\nThe statistics in manufacturing are staggering. Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances in technology.\nThat is why more people are criticizing the ever-increasing role of technology in our economy. Our country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with fewer workers. However, it's not just factories that are seeing losses—software and information technology are also having a dramatic impact on jobs most people think are secure from the forces of a rapidly-changing economy. Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on American families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\nSo why are people so insistent on ignoring the perils of automation? They are failing to look ahead at a time when planning for the future is more important than ever. Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before it. I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers makes me skeptical. No one can currently say where the new jobs are coming from or when, and any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives.\nI'm not overstating the danger: look at what's happened to the labor force. According to economic research, one in six working-age men, 25–54, doesn't have a job. Fifty years ago, nearly 100 percent of men that age were working. Women's labor force participation, meanwhile, has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s.\nAmerican families and prominent business leaders are aware that there's a big problem with automation. The value of a college degree is diminishing, and our upward mobility is declining. If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start thinking about how we can rightfully address automation.", "50": "The subject of automation and its role in our economy has taken hold in American public discourse. Technology broadly and automation specifically are dramatically reshaping the way we work. And we need to have a plan for what's still to come.\nWe don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of automation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood libraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\nThe statistics in manufacturing are staggering. Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances in technology.\nThat is why more people are criticizing the ever-increasing role of technology in our economy. Our country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with fewer workers. However, it's not just factories that are seeing losses—software and information technology are also having a dramatic impact on jobs most people think are secure from the forces of a rapidly-changing economy. Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on American families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\nSo why are people so insistent on ignoring the perils of automation? They are failing to look ahead at a time when planning for the future is more important than ever. Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before it. I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers makes me skeptical. No one can currently say where the new jobs are coming from or when, and any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives.\nI'm not overstating the danger: look at what's happened to the labor force. According to economic research, one in six working-age men, 25–54, doesn't have a job. Fifty years ago, nearly 100 percent of men that age were working. Women's labor force participation, meanwhile, has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s.\nAmerican families and prominent business leaders are aware that there's a big problem with automation. The value of a college degree is diminishing, and our upward mobility is declining. If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start thinking about how we can rightfully address automation.", "51": "Look at the people around you. Some are passive, others more aggressive. Some work best alone, others crave companionship. We easily recognize that there is great variation among the individuals who live near us. Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based on their country of origin.\nStatistics specialists, when they speak of national averages, often make the same mistake.\nNewly published research shows how erroneous such overviews are. Three researchers analyzed decades of values-based surveys and found that only between 16% and 21% of the variation in cultural values could be explained by differences between countries. In other words, the vast majority of what makes us culturally distinct from one another has nothing to do with our homeland.\nTo determine what factors really are associated with culture, the authors combined data from 558 prior surveys that each measured one or more of Hofstede's cultural dimensions. These are traits, such as individualism and masculinity, that describe work-related cultural values. (They are not a measure of visible cultural traits, such as food or dress.) Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the singular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and international comparisons.\nThe researchers found that both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as long-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than nationality. Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\nThe evidence implies that people with similar jobs and incomes are more culturally alike, regardless of where they live. Vas Taras, the lead author of the study, puts it this way: \"Tell me how much you make and I will make a pretty accurate prediction about your cultural values. Tell me what your nationality is and I probably will make a wrong prediction.\"\nTaras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers. Chinese and American lawyers might be trained to interact based on the assumption that the Chinese person is less individualistic, even though their similar social economic situations make it probable they are actually quite alike in that regard.\nThe country, as the unit of authority, is often a convenient way of generalizing about a population.\nHowever, our focus on countries can mask broad variations within them. In the majority of cases we would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income, rather than by the lines surrounding them on a map.", "52": "Look at the people around you. Some are passive, others more aggressive. Some work best alone, others crave companionship. We easily recognize that there is great variation among the individuals who live near us. Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based on their country of origin.\nStatistics specialists, when they speak of national averages, often make the same mistake.\nNewly published research shows how erroneous such overviews are. Three researchers analyzed decades of values-based surveys and found that only between 16% and 21% of the variation in cultural values could be explained by differences between countries. In other words, the vast majority of what makes us culturally distinct from one another has nothing to do with our homeland.\nTo determine what factors really are associated with culture, the authors combined data from 558 prior surveys that each measured one or more of Hofstede's cultural dimensions. These are traits, such as individualism and masculinity, that describe work-related cultural values. (They are not a measure of visible cultural traits, such as food or dress.) Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the singular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and international comparisons.\nThe researchers found that both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as long-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than nationality. Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\nThe evidence implies that people with similar jobs and incomes are more culturally alike, regardless of where they live. Vas Taras, the lead author of the study, puts it this way: \"Tell me how much you make and I will make a pretty accurate prediction about your cultural values. Tell me what your nationality is and I probably will make a wrong prediction.\"\nTaras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers. Chinese and American lawyers might be trained to interact based on the assumption that the Chinese person is less individualistic, even though their similar social economic situations make it probable they are actually quite alike in that regard.\nThe country, as the unit of authority, is often a convenient way of generalizing about a population.\nHowever, our focus on countries can mask broad variations within them. In the majority of cases we would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income, rather than by the lines surrounding them on a map.", "53": "Look at the people around you. Some are passive, others more aggressive. Some work best alone, others crave companionship. We easily recognize that there is great variation among the individuals who live near us. Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based on their country of origin.\nStatistics specialists, when they speak of national averages, often make the same mistake.\nNewly published research shows how erroneous such overviews are. Three researchers analyzed decades of values-based surveys and found that only between 16% and 21% of the variation in cultural values could be explained by differences between countries. In other words, the vast majority of what makes us culturally distinct from one another has nothing to do with our homeland.\nTo determine what factors really are associated with culture, the authors combined data from 558 prior surveys that each measured one or more of Hofstede's cultural dimensions. These are traits, such as individualism and masculinity, that describe work-related cultural values. (They are not a measure of visible cultural traits, such as food or dress.) Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the singular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and international comparisons.\nThe researchers found that both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as long-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than nationality. Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\nThe evidence implies that people with similar jobs and incomes are more culturally alike, regardless of where they live. Vas Taras, the lead author of the study, puts it this way: \"Tell me how much you make and I will make a pretty accurate prediction about your cultural values. Tell me what your nationality is and I probably will make a wrong prediction.\"\nTaras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers. Chinese and American lawyers might be trained to interact based on the assumption that the Chinese person is less individualistic, even though their similar social economic situations make it probable they are actually quite alike in that regard.\nThe country, as the unit of authority, is often a convenient way of generalizing about a population.\nHowever, our focus on countries can mask broad variations within them. In the majority of cases we would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income, rather than by the lines surrounding them on a map.", "54": "Look at the people around you. Some are passive, others more aggressive. Some work best alone, others crave companionship. We easily recognize that there is great variation among the individuals who live near us. Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based on their country of origin.\nStatistics specialists, when they speak of national averages, often make the same mistake.\nNewly published research shows how erroneous such overviews are. Three researchers analyzed decades of values-based surveys and found that only between 16% and 21% of the variation in cultural values could be explained by differences between countries. In other words, the vast majority of what makes us culturally distinct from one another has nothing to do with our homeland.\nTo determine what factors really are associated with culture, the authors combined data from 558 prior surveys that each measured one or more of Hofstede's cultural dimensions. These are traits, such as individualism and masculinity, that describe work-related cultural values. (They are not a measure of visible cultural traits, such as food or dress.) Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the singular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and international comparisons.\nThe researchers found that both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as long-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than nationality. Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\nThe evidence implies that people with similar jobs and incomes are more culturally alike, regardless of where they live. Vas Taras, the lead author of the study, puts it this way: \"Tell me how much you make and I will make a pretty accurate prediction about your cultural values. Tell me what your nationality is and I probably will make a wrong prediction.\"\nTaras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers. Chinese and American lawyers might be trained to interact based on the assumption that the Chinese person is less individualistic, even though their similar social economic situations make it probable they are actually quite alike in that regard.\nThe country, as the unit of authority, is often a convenient way of generalizing about a population.\nHowever, our focus on countries can mask broad variations within them. In the majority of cases we would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income, rather than by the lines surrounding them on a map.", "55": "Look at the people around you. Some are passive, others more aggressive. Some work best alone, others crave companionship. We easily recognize that there is great variation among the individuals who live near us. Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based on their country of origin.\nStatistics specialists, when they speak of national averages, often make the same mistake.\nNewly published research shows how erroneous such overviews are. Three researchers analyzed decades of values-based surveys and found that only between 16% and 21% of the variation in cultural values could be explained by differences between countries. In other words, the vast majority of what makes us culturally distinct from one another has nothing to do with our homeland.\nTo determine what factors really are associated with culture, the authors combined data from 558 prior surveys that each measured one or more of Hofstede's cultural dimensions. These are traits, such as individualism and masculinity, that describe work-related cultural values. (They are not a measure of visible cultural traits, such as food or dress.) Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the singular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and international comparisons.\nThe researchers found that both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as long-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than nationality. Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\nThe evidence implies that people with similar jobs and incomes are more culturally alike, regardless of where they live. Vas Taras, the lead author of the study, puts it this way: \"Tell me how much you make and I will make a pretty accurate prediction about your cultural values. Tell me what your nationality is and I probably will make a wrong prediction.\"\nTaras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers. Chinese and American lawyers might be trained to interact based on the assumption that the Chinese person is less individualistic, even though their similar social economic situations make it probable they are actually quite alike in that regard.\nThe country, as the unit of authority, is often a convenient way of generalizing about a population.\nHowever, our focus on countries can mask broad variations within them. In the majority of cases we would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income, rather than by the lines surrounding them on a map."}, "questions": [{"id": "2021-12-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to the author, humans can hardly survive if separated from their community.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】脱离社群难以生存\n【定位】Becoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a death sentence.\n和部落分开——或更糟，被驱逐——等于被判了死刑。\n【改写】separated from their community ↔\nBecoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out\ncommunity 是 tribe 的上位词\ncan hardly survive ↔ was a death sentence\n死刑=不能存活的程度化同义\n【辨邻】 C C 段只讲到 Humans need a reasonably accurate view of the world in order to\nsurvive，谈的是生存与世界观的关系，没有 separated/community/death 这条主\n线，只覆盖陈述句一半。\nHumans need a reasonably accurate view of the world in order to survive", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "People often accept false beliefs because they prioritize social bonds rather than facts.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】优先社交纽带而非事实\n【定位】When we have to choose between the two, people often select friends and family over facts.\n当我们必须在两者之间选择时，人们往往选择朋友与家人而非事实。\n【改写】prioritize social bonds rather than facts ↔ people often select friends and family over facts\n二选一句式同义改写\naccept false beliefs ↔\nFalse beliefs can be useful in a social sense even if they are not useful in a factual sense\n采信错误信念在两侧呼应\n【辨邻】 F F 段讲 We don't always believe things because they are correct. Sometimes we\nbelieve things because they make us look good，话题与社交动机相邻，但没有\nfriends and family vs facts 这种二选一表态，与题干 prioritize…rather than… 的句\n式锚不上。\nSometimes we believe things because they make us look good to the people we care about\nE E 段讲 social connection is actually more helpful to your daily life than\nunderstanding the truth，侧重日常中社交连接对生活的助益（对应第 41 题），不\n是题干强调的 accept false beliefs 这条因果。\nsocial connection is actually more helpful to your daily life than understanding the truth of a\nparticular fact or idea", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Most often people learn from those close to them.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】从亲近者处学到最多\n【定位】The most heated arguments often occur between people on opposite ends of the spectrum, but\nthe most frequent learning occurs from people who are nearby.\n最激烈的争论往往发生在光谱两端的人之间，但最频繁的学习发生在彼此相近的人之间。\n【改写】Most often people learn ↔ the most frequent learning occurs\nmost often 与 most frequent 同义\nthose close to them ↔ people who are nearby\nclose 与 nearby 同义\n【辨邻】 K K 段说 The people who are most likely to change our minds are the ones we\nagree with on 98 percent of topics，主词是 change our minds，而题干 Q38 的主\n词是 learn，并非同一动作。\nThe people who are most likely to change our minds are the ones we agree with on 98 percent\nof topics\nL L 段说 your time is better spent connecting with people who are at Positions\n6 and 8，强调的是与邻位的人 connect，没有 learn from 这一动作，与题干主词\n错位。\nyour time is better spent connecting with people who are at Positions 6 and 8, gradually pulling\nthem in your direction", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Sometimes people adopt certain beliefs in order to leave a favorable impression on those dear to them.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】为给亲近者留好印象而采信\n【定位】Sometimes we believe things because they make us look good to the people we care about.\n有时候我们相信某些事，是因为这些事让我们在我们在乎的人面前显得好看。\n【改写】adopt certain beliefs ↔ we believe things\nadopt beliefs 与 believe things 同义\nleave a favorable impression ↔ make us look good\n留下好印象 = 让自己显得好看\nthose dear to them ↔ the people we care about\n亲近的人 = 在乎的人\n【辨邻】 G G 段重点是 select friends and family over facts，强调在事实与亲友间选择的结果，\n而题干 Q39 强调的是 为留好印象 这一动机，落脚不同。\nWhen we have to choose between the two, people often select friends and family over facts\nE E 段说 hold beliefs that bring the belief-holder the greatest number of allies,\nprotectors, or disciples，谈的是争取盟友数量最大化，不是题干强调的 在所亲近者\n面前留好印象 这层亲密关系动机。\nhold beliefs that bring the belief-holder the greatest number of allies, protectors, or disciples", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Compared with face-to-face communication, books often provide a better medium for changing people's beliefs.", "options": null, "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】书比面对面交流更利于改变信念\n【定位】As a result, books are often a better vehicle for transforming beliefs than conversations\nor debates.\n结果是，比起对话或辩论，书往往是一种更好的改变信念的载体。\n【改写】books often provide a better medium ↔ books are often a better vehicle\nmedium 与 vehicle 同义\nchanging people's beliefs ↔ transforming beliefs\nchanging 与 transforming 同义\nface-to-face communication ↔ conversations or debates\n上位概括", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "On many occasions in daily life, people benefit more from their social bonds than from knowing the truth.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】日常中社交纽带比知道真相更有益\n【定位】In many circumstances, social connection is actually more helpful to your daily life than\nunderstanding the truth of a particular fact or idea.\n在许多情形下，社交连接实际上比理解某一具体事实或观念的真相更有助于你的日常生活。\n【改写】On many occasions in daily life ↔ In many circumstances\n频次状语同义对换\nbenefit more from their social bonds than from knowing the truth ↔\nsocial connection is actually more helpful to your daily life than understanding the truth of a\nparticular fact or idea\n比较句式重构\n【辨邻】 C C 段说 truth and accuracy are not the only things that matter to the human\nmind. Humans also seem to have a deep desire to belong，只提出两种需要并列\n存在，并未在日常情境下把社交连接置于真相之上。\ntruth and accuracy are not the only things that matter to the human mind. Humans also seem to have\na deep desire to belong\nG G 段强调 When we have to choose between the two, people often select friends\nand family over facts，是被迫二选一时的选择，不是题干说的 在许多日常情形下 受\n益更多。\nWhen we have to choose between the two, people often select friends and family over facts", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "If you want to change somebody's beliefs, you should first establish social connection with them.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】想改信念先建立社交连接\n【定位】The way to change people's minds is to become friends with them, to integrate them into your\ntribe, to bring them into your circle.\n改变别人想法的方式是与他们成为朋友，把他们融入你的部落，把他们带进你的圈子。\n【改写】change somebody's beliefs ↔ change people's minds\nbeliefs 与 minds 在本文同义\nfirst establish social connection ↔\nbecome friends with them, to integrate them into your tribe, to bring them into your circle\n上位概括\n【辨邻】 H H 段说 You can't expect someone to change their mind if you take away their\ncommunity too. You have to give them somewhere to go，落脚是 别抽走他的群\n体，与题干 first establish social connection 的正向先后步骤不一致。\nYou can't expect someone to change their mind if you take away their community too\nK K 段强调 Facts don't change our minds. Friendship does，回答的是 谁能改变想法\n（友谊），不是题干问的 想改变想法你应先做什么（先建立社交连接）。\nFacts don't change our minds. Friendship does", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Humans cannot survive without a fair knowledge of the actual world.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】无对真实世界的认识则无法生存\n【定位】Humans need a reasonably accurate view of the world in order to survive.\n人类要生存，就需要一种对世界相当准确的认识。\n【改写】Humans cannot survive without a fair knowledge of the actual world ↔\nHumans need a reasonably accurate view of the world in order to survive\n双重否定与肯定同义\n【辨邻】 D D 段说 Becoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a\ndeath sentence，落脚是社群关系对生存的意义，与题干 a fair knowledge of the\nactual world 这一认知层面无关。\nBecoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a death sentence", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Repetition of bad ideas increases their chances of being accepted.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】重复坏想法增加被接受机会\n【定位】The more you repeat a bad idea, the more likely people are to believe it.\n你越是重复一个坏想法，人们就越可能相信它。\n【改写】Repetition of bad ideas ↔ repeat a bad idea\n名词化\nincreases their chances of being accepted ↔ the more likely people are to believe it\n比较句式与可能性同义\n【辨邻】 P P 段说 The number of people who believe an idea is directly proportional to the\nnumber of times it has been repeated during the last year，是把 O 段结论命名为\nClear's Law of Recurrence，是 给命题命名 而非首次提出 重复→被接受 这一命题。\n题干 Q44 表述的是命题本身，首发在 O 段。\nThe number of people who believe an idea is directly proportional to the number of times it has\nbeen repeated during the last year", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Nobody is willing to give up their beliefs at the risk of getting isolated.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】不愿冒孤立之险而放弃信念\n【定位】Nobody wants their worldview torn apart if loneliness is the outcome.\n如果结果是孤独，没人愿意让自己的世界观被撕裂。\n【改写】Nobody is willing to give up their beliefs ↔ Nobody wants their worldview torn apart\n放弃信念 = 让世界观被撕裂\nat the risk of getting isolated ↔ if loneliness is the outcome\n冒孤立之险 = 若结果是孤独\n【辨邻】 I I 段说 they can change their beliefs without the risk of being abandoned\nsocially，落脚是 提供方法让人能改信念，与题干 Q45 的 没人愿意放弃信念 这一反向\n心理表述不一致。\nthey can change their beliefs without the risk of being abandoned socially\nD D 段说 Becoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a\ndeath sentence，落脚是 被部落驱逐的生死后果，与题干 Q45 的 不愿放弃信念以免\n孤立 这一信念—孤立链条不同。\nBecoming separated from the tribe—or worse, being cast out—was a death sentence", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What can we observe from the author's description of our communities?", "options": ["A) The growing passion for automation.", "B) The shift from manual jobs to IT ones.", "C) Their changing views on employment.", "D) Their fading employment opportunities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What can we observe from the author's description of our communities 问的是从作\n者描述中能 \"观察到\" 什么，是基于原文事实的客观提取，不含 infer/imply 等推断信号。\n【拆句】题干主干 What can we observe from the author's description of our communities，\n主语 we、谓语 observe、宾语为 what；介词短语 from the author's description of our\ncommunities 是状语，提示 observe 的依据来源。结构平铺，无陷阱。\n【定位】We don't have to look further than our own communities to see the devastating impact of\nautomation. From automated warehouses to cashierless grocery stores to neighborhood\nlibraries that offer self-checkout lanes instead of employing real people—automation is\nincreasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind.\n无需舍近求远，从我们自己的社区就能看到自动化的破坏性影响。从自动化仓库、无人收银\n的杂货店，到提供自助结账通道而不再雇真人的社区图书馆——自动化正在不断取代工作岗\n位，留给人们的好的新岗位太少。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThe growing passion for automation 称社区对自动化抱有越来越大的热情，但原文\nthe devastating impact of automation 是负面描述，没有任何 passion（热情）的\n成分。\nB) 无中生有\nThe shift from manual jobs to IT ones 提出岗位类型在 \"体力→IT\" 之间转移，但 P2\n只说岗位被取代、好新岗不足，并未说明流向 IT；该说法在文中无依据。\nC) 答非所问\nTheir changing views on employment 谈的是 \"对就业的看法在变\"，但题干问的是\ndescription 描绘出的实际状况，且 P2 并未讨论 views（观念）的变化。\nD) ✓ 正确\nautomation is increasingly replacing jobs and leaving too few good new jobs behind ↔\nTheir fading employment opportunities\n预判答案的核心语义是 \"岗位机会在消逝\"，四个选项中只有 fading employment\nopportunities 与之严丝合缝。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from a recent report?", "options": ["A) The manufacturing sector is declining at a fast rate.", "B) The concerns about the effect of trade are exaggerated.", "C) The fears about trade have been spreading far and wide.", "D) The impact of trade on employment has been staggering."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from a recent report 直接问 report 里说了什么，是对原文事实的\n转述。\n【拆句】What do we learn from a recent report，疑问句结构 What + 助动词 do + 主语 we +\n谓语 learn + 介词短语 from a recent report；无从句、无否定，理解无障碍。\n【定位】Despite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs\nlost in manufacturing are due to trade—the rest of the losses have been due to advances\nin technology.\n尽管人们对贸易普遍感到担忧，最近一份报告显示，制造业的岗位流失中仅 13% 归因于贸\n易——其余则归因于技术进步。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe manufacturing sector is declining at a fast rate 称制造业整体在快速衰退，但\nOur country is manufacturing more than ever before, but we are doing it with\nfewer workers 显示制造业产出反而 \"前所未有的多\"，并非 declining。\nB) ✓ 正确\nDespite the widespread fears about trade, a recent report showed that just 13 percent of jobs lost\nin manufacturing are due to trade\n↔ The concerns about the effect of trade are exaggerated\n把预判 \"担忧言过其实\" 与四个选项一比，只有 exaggerated 与之同义。\nC) 答非所问\nThe fears about trade have been spreading far and wide 描述的是恐惧本身在扩\n散，但 P3 报告内容是 \"只有 13% 归因于贸易\"，并非讲恐惧的传播范围；且\nwidespread 是状态而非 \"正在扩散\"。\nD) 与原文相反\nThe impact of trade on employment has been staggering 说贸易对就业影响 \"惊人\n之大\"，但 just 13 percent…are due to trade 表明贸易影响极小；staggering 反而是\n文中用于形容 \"statistics in manufacturing\" 的词，被错位挪用。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What does the passage tell us about American workers in an era of transformation?", "options": ["A) They feel ignored by politicians.", "B) They feel increasingly vulnerable.", "C) They keep adapting to the changes.", "D) They keep complaining but to no avail."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the passage tell us about American workers 是询问文章对工人状况的客观\n陈述，不涉及推理。\n【拆句】What does the passage tell us about American workers in an era of\ntransformation：疑问句主干 the passage tell us about American workers；介词短语\nin an era of transformation 是 workers 的后置定语，限定 workers 所处状态。\n【定位】Something transformative is happening in America that is having an adverse effect on\nAmerican families. Whether policymakers and politicians admit it or not, workers have made\nclear their feelings about their economic insecurity and desire to keep good jobs in America.\n美国正在发生某种变革性变化，对美国家庭产生不利影响。无论政策制定者和政客们是否承\n认，工人们已经清楚地表达了他们对自身经济上不安全的感受，以及希望留住国内好工作的\n愿望。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThey feel ignored by politicians 说工人觉得被政客忽视；原文 Whether\npolicymakers and politicians admit it or not 只是让步状语，意思是 \"无论政客是否承\n认\"，并未说工人 feel ignored；这是把 admit it or not 误读为 ignored。\nB) ✓ 正确\nworkers have made clear their feelings about their economic insecurity ↔\nThey feel increasingly vulnerable\n预判的核心 \"经济上不安全\" 与 vulnerable（易受伤、脆弱的）语义同义；四个选项中只\n有 B 落在 \"工人感受\" 这一维度上。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey keep adapting to the changes 称工人在不断适应变化；但 P4 强调工人\neconomic insecurity 与 desire to keep good jobs，是 \"抗拒变化、希望保住\"，与\n\"adapting\" 方向相反。\nD) 过度推断\nThey keep complaining but to no avail 说工人不断抱怨却毫无效果；原文 made\nclear their feelings 是 \"表达感受\"，并非 complaining；更无 \"无果\" 的结论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author think of automation?", "options": ["A) It will have the same impact as industrialization.", "B) It provides sensible companies with alternatives.", "C) Its alleged positive effects are doubtful.", "D) Its detrimental effects are unavoidable."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of automation 直接问作者对自动化的看法，是观点/态度题\n的标准题干。\n【拆句】What does the author think of automation：主干 the author think of automation，\nthink of 在此意为 \"对…的看法\"。结构简单，但要小心 think of 是固定短语，等同于 think\nabout / view。\n【定位】I sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave\nthe way for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on\nworkers makes me skeptical.\n我真诚地希望那些断言自动化将让我们更高效、为新职业铺路的人是对的，但自动化对工人\n的不利影响这一现实让我心存疑虑。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt will have the same impact as industrialization 称自动化与工业化影响相同；但原\n文 Resisting automation is futile: it is as inevitable as industrialization was before\nit 只用 industrialization 作 \"不可避免性\" 的类比，并未说两者 impact 一样。\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt provides sensible companies with alternatives 说自动化为公司提供备选方案；原\n文 any sensible company or country should prepare for all alternatives 是公司 \"应\n自己准备好应对各种可能\"，主语错位（公司主动准备 → 选项变成自动化提供）。\nC) ✓ 正确\nI sincerely hope that those who assert that automation will make us more effective and pave the\nway for new occupations are right, but the reality of automation's detrimental effects on workers\nmakes me skeptical\n↔ Its alleged positive effects are doubtful\n预判语义 \"所谓正面效果不可靠\" 与 C 字面几乎一对一对应。\nD) 过度推断\nIts detrimental effects are unavoidable 称 \"不利影响不可避免\"；原文 it is as\ninevitable as industrialization 说的是 \"自动化本身不可避免\"，被诱导嫁接到\ndetrimental effects 上；且作者重点是 skeptical（怀疑乐观说法），而非给\ndetrimental effects 下 \"不可避免\" 的定论。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What should we attach importance to when dealing with automation?", "options": ["A) College graduates' job prospects.", "B) Women's access to employment.", "C) People's economic security.", "D) People's social mobility."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What should we attach importance to when dealing with automation 问的是应重视的\n具体方面（某个对象/某种状态），是对原文呼吁句的细节提取，不是全文主旨。\n【拆句】What should we attach importance to when dealing with automation：主干 we\nshould attach importance to what；when dealing with automation 是时间状语从句\n（省略主语 we）。attach importance to 为固定搭配 = 重视。\n【定位】If we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start\nthinking about how we can rightfully address automation.\n如果我们想要一个让每个人都能在经济上获得保障的经济体，我们就需要开始思考如何妥善\n应对自动化。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nCollege graduates' job prospects 把大学毕业生的就业前景当成应重视的对象；但\nThe value of a college degree is diminishing 只是用大学学位贬值作论据，并未将\n\"大学毕业生就业前景\" 提升为应重视的核心。\nB) 以偏概全\nWomen's access to employment 把 \"女性就业机会\" 当应重视对象；Women's labor\nforce participation…has slipped back to the level it was at in the late 1980s 只是\n数据例证，用以佐证劳动力问题，并非作者要重视的核心对象。\nC) ✓ 正确\nIf we want an economy that allows everyone to be economically secure, we need to start\nthinking about how we can rightfully address automation\n↔ People's economic security\n在四个选项中，只有 C 与预判语义完全等值，其他三个都是文中曾出现但未被作者 \"郑\n重提出\" 的对象。\nD) 偷换概念\nPeople's social mobility 把 our upward mobility is declining 中的 upward mobility\n改成 social mobility。upward mobility 强调 \"向上流动\"，social mobility 是更宽泛的\n\"社会流动\"；且作者只把它列为现象之一，并未提升为应重视的核心。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What error do experts often make when describing people from other places?", "options": ["A) They tend to overly rely on nationality.", "B) They often exaggerate their differences.", "C) They often misunderstand their cultures.", "D) They tend to dwell on national averages."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What error do experts often make when describing people from other places? 是\n问\"什么错误\"——指向文中明确陈述的事实，没有 infer/imply 一类词。\n【拆句】Q51 主干是 What error do experts often make（experts 是主语、make 是谓语、error\n前移作疑问宾语），后接时间状语从句 when describing people from other places\n（when + 现在分词，省略 they are）。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Yet, when we speak of people from elsewhere, we seem to inevitably characterize them based\non their country of origin.\n然而，当我们谈论来自他处的人时，我们似乎总是不可避免地根据其原籍国来描述他们。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncharacterize them based on their country of origin ↔ overly rely on nationality\n预判落在\"按国别给外国人贴标签\"上，A 选项的 overly rely on nationality 把\"按 X 归\n类 + 不可避免\"两层意思干净打包，与预判完全对应。\nB) 无中生有\nexaggerate their differences 原文从未出现；P1 强调的是\"国别概括\" vs \"个体差异\"，\n不是\"夸大\"或\"缩小\"差异。\nC) 偷换概念\nmisunderstand their cultures 把\"按国别概括\"偷换成\"误解文化\"；P1 的错误是\"贴国别\n标签\"这个动作本身，至于\"是否误解了文化内容\"，P1 并没有说。\nD) 张冠李戴\ndwell on national averages 出自 Statistics specialists, when they speak of national\naverages——national averages 是\"统计专家\"特有的工具，不是泛指的\"专家\"在描\n述\"外国人\"时的通病。题干虽用 experts，但承接的是 P1 \"我们\"的归类错误，P2 只是\n补充统计专家也犯\"同一个\"错；这个\"同一个错\"本质是\"按国别归类\"，而不是\"沉迷国家\n平均值\"——national averages 只是统计专家展现这一错误的具体形式。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What do we learn about Hofstede's cultural dimensions?", "options": ["A) They are useful in comparing cultural values across time and space.", "B) They have brought unusual benefits to people of different cultures.", "C) They are widely used to identify people's individual traits.", "D) They provide valuable questions for researchers to study."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about Hofstede's cultural dimensions? 问的是关于 X 我们\"了解到\"什\n么，指向文中对该术语的客观陈述。\n【拆句】Q52 主干 What do we learn（疑问宾语前移），介词短语 about Hofstede's cultural\ndimensions 限定话题。结构简单，无陷阱。\n【定位】Though the validity of Hofstede's dimensions has been questioned, they have the\nsingular benefit of having been in use for decades, which allows for historical and\ninternational comparisons.\n虽然 Hofstede 文化维度的有效性受到过质疑，但它有一个独到的优势——已被使用了数十\n年，这使得历史性和国际性的比较成为可能。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nallows for historical and international comparisons ↔\nuseful in comparing cultural values across time and space\nhaving been in use for decades ↔ useful\n预判与 A 的核心信息在两个维度（时间/空间）和功能（comparing）上一一对应，无\n多余、无遗漏。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文说该维度本身 have the singular benefit（\"自身具备这一优势\"，指研究工具属\n性），而 brought unusual benefits to people of different cultures 把受益方偷换\n成\"不同文化的人\"——文中从未说该维度给\"不同文化的人\"带来过好处。\nC) 与原文相反\nidentify people's individual traits 与 P4 的 They are not a measure of visible\ncultural traits, such as food or dress. 不完全冲突，但 P4 明确 Hofstede 维度衡量的\n是\"工作相关的文化价值\"，是\"群体/文化层面\"的 traits，而非\"个人特质\"。把\"群体文化\n价值\"偷换成\"个人特质\"，方向相反。\nD) 无中生有\nprovide valuable questions for researchers to study 在原文中找不到任何对应——\nP4 只说该维度本身被研究者使用、能做比较，没说它\"提供研究问题\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What did researchers find about previous studies on factors determining people's values?", "options": ["A) Environmental factors were prioritized over other factors.", "B) An individual's financial status was often underestimated.", "C) Too much emphasis had been placed on one's occupation.", "D) The impact of social progress on one's values was ignored."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did researchers find about previous studies 问\"研究者发现了什么\"，对应原文\nthe researchers found that... 一类直接陈述。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What did researchers find，介词短语 about previous studies on factors\ndetermining people's values 三层嵌套：先讲 previous studies，再讲这些研究关于\nfactors，最后讲这些 factors 是 determining people's values 的。基础薄弱考生易把焦点\n错认成 previous studies 本身，其实焦点在最里层的 factors。\n【定位】Occupation and social economic status were the most strongly correlated, suggesting that our\nvalues are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for.\n职业和社会经济地位的相关性最强，这表明我们的价值观更多地是由经济因素驱动的，超出\n了我们平时给它的认可程度。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nP5 说 both demographic factors, such as age, and environmental factors, such as\nlong-term unemployment rates, were more correlated with cultural values than\nnationality——environmental factors 只是\"比国籍更相关\"的因素之一，且不是\"最\n强\"；prioritized over other factors 把它说成\"凌驾于其他因素之上\"，与\"最强相关其实\n是 occupation 和 social economic status\"相反。\nB) ✓ 正确\nour values are more economically driven than we usually give them credit for ↔\nAn individual's financial status was often underestimated\nOccupation and social economic status ↔ An individual's financial status\n预判与 B 的两个核心要素\"经济维度\"和\"被低估\"都精确对应，无偏。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 suggesting that values are more economically driven than we usually give\nthem credit for 表明 occupation/social economic status 一直被\"低估\"——而 Too\nmuch emphasis had been placed on one's occupation 说\"过度强调\"，方向反转。\nD) 无中生有\nThe impact of social progress on one's values was ignored 中的 social progress 全\n文未出现；P5 只列了 demographic、environmental、occupation、social\neconomic status 等 factor，没出现 social progress 这个术语。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the impact on employees when cultures are identified with countries?", "options": ["A) They may fail to see the cultural biases of their business partners.", "B) They may fail to attach sufficient importance to cultural diversity.", "C) They may not be taught how to properly interact with overseas partners.", "D) They may not be able to learn the legal procedures for business transactions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is the impact on employees when cultures are identified with countries? 问\"产\n生什么影响\"，对应原文中 caused / make / lead to 一类因果直陈句。\n【拆句】Q54 主干 What is the impact on employees，时间状语从句 when cultures are\nidentified with countries（被动语态 are identified with，意为\"被等同于\"）。被动语态\n可能让基础薄弱考生分不清主客体，要记住\"文化\"被等同于\"国家\"，而不是反过来。\n【定位】Taras says our erroneous belief that countries are cultures has caused businesses to teach their\nemployees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers.\nTaras 说，我们\"国家即文化\"的错误信念已经导致企业教给员工无用甚至有害的与国际同行\n的相处方式。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nfail to see the cultural biases of their business partners 中的\"文化偏见\"和\"看不\n出\"原文均未提及；P7 谈的是\"员工被教错了方法\"，不是\"员工没能识别伙伴的偏见\"。\nB) 偷换概念\nfail to attach sufficient importance to cultural diversity 把考点偷换成\"对文化多样性\n不够重视\"——而 P7 谴责的恰恰是\"按国家归类时，假定差异（如中国人不够个人主\n义）\"，问题是\"错把差异本质化\"，而非\"忽视多样性\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nteach their employees useless or even harmful ways of interacting with their international peers\n↔ may not be taught how to properly interact with overseas partners\n\"学到无用方式\"等于\"没学到正确方式\"——把同一事实的肯定—正面表述和否定—正面\n表述对得上，就锁定 C。\nD) 无中生有\nlearn the legal procedures for business transactions 利用 P7 例子里的 Chinese\nand American lawyers 这一职业关键词，生造出\"法律程序\"——但律师只是用来举\n例\"中美专业人士因经济地位相似而其实很像\"，与\"法律程序\"无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2021-12-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author suggest at the end of the passage?", "options": ["A) There is sufficient reason to generalize about a country's population.", "B) The majority of people are still constrained by their national identity.", "C) It is arguable that the country should be regarded as the unit of authority.", "D) Nationality is less useful than socio-economic status as an indicator of one's values."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （推断题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest at the end of the passage? 含 suggest 且指向作者，问\n的是末段流露出的作者立场。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What does the author suggest，位置状语 at the end of the passage。结构\n平铺，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nsufficient reason to generalize about a country's population 与作者立场完全相\n反；P8 只承认 convenient（便利），而 P9 紧接着用 However 否定这种概括是\"足\n够的\"。\"convenient\"和\"sufficient reason\"差距巨大——前者只是方便，后者是充分\n依据。\nB) 偷换概念\nThe majority of people are still constrained by their national identity 把 P9 的 the\nfactors that constrain their lives（约束他们生活的因素）中的 constrain 一词截下，\n再换上 national identity 作主语——但原文 constrain 的施动者是 income 等\"经济\n因素\"，不是\"国家身份\"；作者要表达的是\"按经济因素识别更好\"，而不是\"人被国籍\n约束\"。\nC) 无中生有\nIt is arguable that the country should be regarded as the unit of authority 抓住\nP8 的 the unit of authority（权威单位） 做诱饵——但 P8 用这个表述只是描述\"国家\n是一种概括方式\"，作者并没有在末段\"质疑国家是否应当被视为权威单位\"这个抽象问题\n上展开。这是借原文具体术语生造出新的辩题。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwe would be better off identifying people by the factors that constrain their lives, like income,\nrather than by the lines surrounding them on a map\n↔ Nationality is less useful than socio-economic status as an indicator of one's values\n预判与 D 在比较的两端（国籍 vs 社会经济地位）和比较方向（前者不如后者）上完全\n一致。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2021-12-1", "clozeSharedWith": "2021-12-1"});
